REGIONAL REGULATION OF INDRAMAYU REGENCY

NUMBER 18 YEAR 2012

CONCERNING

PREVENTION, PROTECTION AND RECOVERY OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN AS VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE IN INDRAMAYU REGENCY

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

THE REGENT OF INDRAMAYU,

Considering:

a. that violence against women and children is an act that violates the rights and dignity of women and children as human beings;
b. that the implementation of prevention, protection and recovery of women and children as victims of violence in Indramayu Regency has not been carried out optimally;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a and letter b above, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Prevention, Protection and Recovery of Women and Children as Victims of Violence in Indramayu Regency.

In view of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 14 Year 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1950) as amended by Law Number 4 Year 1968 concerning the Establishment of Purwakarta Regency and Subang Regency by Amending Law Number 14 Year 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1968 Number 31, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2851);
3. Law Number 7 Year 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3668);
4. Law Number 39 Year 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 Year 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 169, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
6. Law Number 23 Year 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
7. Law Number 32 Year 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437) as amended for the second time by Law Number 12 Year 2008 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 32 Year 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
8. Government Regulation Number 4 Year 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
9. Government Regulation Number 38 Year 2007 concerning Division of Government Affairs Between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
10. Government Regulation Number 41 Year 2007 concerning Regional Apparatus Organization (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 89, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4741);
11. Government Regulation Number 9 Year 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of Human Trafficking Crimes (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
12. Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 7 Year 2008 concerning Regional Secretariat and DPRD Secretariat of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Year 2008 Number 7 Series D.3);
13. Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 8 Year 2008 concerning Regional Agencies of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Year 2008 Number 8 Series: D 4);
14. Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 9 Year 2008 concerning Regional Technical Institutions and Civil Service Police Unit of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Year 2008 Number 9 Series D.5) as amended for the second time by Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 16 Year 2012 concerning the Second Amendment to Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 9 Year 2008 concerning Regional Technical Institutions and Civil Service Police Unit of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Year 2012 Number 16).
15. Regional Regulation of Indramayu Regency Number 5 Year 2012 concerning Procedures for the Establishment of Regional Legal Products of Indramayu Regency (Regional Gazette of Indramayu Regency Number 5 Year 2012).

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF INDRAMAYU REGENCY

and

THE REGENT OF INDRAMAYU

HAS DECIDED:

To Enact:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PREVENTION, PROTECTION AND RECOVERY OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN AS VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE IN INDRAMAYU REGENCY.

Article 3

The purpose of implementing prevention, protection and recovery of women and children as victims of violence is to provide prevention and protection covering the aspects of:
a. Prevention;
b. Service and assistance;
c. Recovery; and
d. Empowerment.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 4

Every victim has the right to obtain:
a. Protection;
b. Information;
c. Minimum service in accordance with Minimum Service Standards (SPM);
d. Continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. Confidential handling;
f. Psychological and legal assistance; and
g. Guarantee of rights related to status as a family member and community member.

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND AUTHORITY

REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

Article 5

1. The Regional Government is obliged to carry out efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence against women and children in the form of:
a. collecting data and information about women and children victims of violence;
b. disseminating laws and regulations related to the implementation of prevention, protection and recovery of women and children as victims of violence and exploitation;
c. disseminating understanding and anti-violence values against Women and Children through IEC (communication, information and education) through communities, schools and universities;
d. monitoring and evaluating the implementation of prevention, protection and recovery of women and children as victims of violence and exploitation.
2. The Regional Government in anticipating the occurrence of acts of violence against Women and Children, has the authority to provide and organize services for victims in the form of:
a. providing and facilitating the establishment of integrated service institutions for victims of violence by involving institutions and community elements;
b. encouraging community awareness of the importance of protection for victims of violence;
c. empowering communities for the prevention of acts of violence.
3. The obligations and authority of the Regional Government are carried out by the Agency

Article 9

Forms of services for victims organized by P2TP2A include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medico-legal services constitute a form of medical service for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. Psychological services constitute services provided in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and to provide social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength and independence in resolving their problems;
d. Legal services to assist victims in undergoing legal processes;
e. Economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.

Article 12

The implementation of prevention, protection and recovery for victims of acts of violence is carried out with:
a. Fast, safe, empathetic;
b. Guarantee of confidentiality;
c. Easily accessible; and
d. No fees charged.

Article 14

Forms of recovery for women and child victims carried out by P2TP2A can be implemented through:
a. rehabilitation and assistance activities are carried out for a maximum of one month;
b. P2TP2A is obliged to provide competent resources such as psychiatrists and psychologists;
c. P2TP2A is obliged to provide physical and psychological health tests for victims of acts of violence;

Article 16

Guidance, control and supervision as referred to in Chapter VII are directed to:
a. increase the level of public awareness so as to be able to prevent, protect and/or restore the physical and or psychological condition of women and children victims of acts of violence;
b. fulfillment of the community's need for information and protection services for women and children victims of acts of violence;
c. provide facilities in order to support the improvement of efforts to implement prevention, protection, recovery of women and children victims of acts of violence;
d. improve the quality of health for women and children who experience acts of violence.

CHAPTER IX

REPORTING

Article 18

1. P2TP2A is obliged to report the implementation of prevention, protection and recovery of women and children victims of acts of violence to the Regent.
2. Reporting as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. Administration;
b. Finance;
c. Services;
d. Performance.
3. Submission of written reports as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out at least once every 3 (three) months.

CHAPTER XII

INVESTIGATION

Article 21

1. Certain Civil Servant Officials within the Regional Government are granted special authority as Investigators to conduct investigations of criminal acts, as referred to in the Criminal Procedure Code.
2. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) are certain civil servant officials within the Regional Government appointed by authorized officials in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. The authority of Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) is:
a. to receive, search for, collect, and examine information or reports concerning criminal acts so that such information or reports become more complete and clear;
b. to examine, search for, and collect information regarding individuals or Legal Entities concerning the truth of acts committed in connection with criminal acts;
c. to request information and evidence from individuals or Legal Entities in connection with criminal acts;
d. to examine books, records, and other documents concerning criminal acts;
e. to conduct searches to obtain evidence of bookkeeping, recording, and other documents, as well as to seize such evidence;
f. to request the assistance of experts in the framework of carrying out criminal investigation duties;
g. to order someone to stop and/or prohibit someone from leaving a room or place when an examination is in progress and to examine the identity of persons, objects, and/or documents carried;
h. to photograph persons related to criminal acts;
i. to summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
j. to terminate investigations; and/or
k. to take other actions necessary for the smooth conduct of criminal investigations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) shall notify the commencement of investigations and submit the results of their investigations to the Public Prosecutor through the Investigator official of the Indonesian National Police, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Code.
